implementationPlan.description:
Contract docs (docs/MCP_SERVER.md). Edit only the moe.claim_next_task, moe.join_team, moe.chat_who and moe.list_workers sections.

First run git diff -- docs/MCP_SERVER.md. As of 2026-10-27 it already carries another task's unlanded hunks: task-cd2e4477's epic finalTaskId text near get_context and create_epic/update_epic. Its landing 4137865 held them because task-72184c65 contests the file. Never revert, reformat or stage those hunks, and name them in your step note. Make your edits with the built-in Edit tool, which is the wrapper's TOOL witness for a contested, already-dirty path.

1. claim_next_task. Add to the Parameters block: workerType?: string // the seat's CLI: CLAUDE | CODEX | GEMINI | GROK. Add a paragraph **Reported worker type (optional):** covering:
- names are exact and case-sensitive;
- any other supplied value (another case, an unknown name, blank, null, a non-string) is recorded as CLAUDE and never refused;
- omitted: a record this claim creates is CLAUDE, and an existing record keeps its type;
- a claim that passes input validation refreshes an existing record BEFORE it answers. That covers a claim, a resume, alreadyAssigned, no work, the governor redirect, and a refusal after validation. It changes only type, never the assignment, attempt, status or lastActivityAt, and it writes only when the value changes;
- the refresh never creates a record; auto-registration still happens only on a successful claim;
- a claim refused for its input (missing or non-claimable statuses, a malformed runner identity) writes nothing;
- a failed type write is logged and does not fail the claim;
- the field is informational only: it is unauthenticated, any caller can report any value, and nothing is gated on it.
2. join_team. Add workerType?: string to the Parameters block. The revive clause '(type, current task and chat cursors kept)' must now say that current task and chat cursors are kept and the type follows a supplied workerType. State that:
- a successful join records the reported type on a new or an existing record, with the same normalization (omitted: CLAUDE for a new record, unchanged for an existing one);
- it changes no current task, status, membership or lastActivityAt;
- an unknown team is refused before any write;
- a full team is refused after a missing record was auto-registered (as before), and an existing record keeps its type.
3. chat_who. Returns becomes { online: Array<{ workerId, type, status, lastActivity, currentTaskId, source }> }. Add a note that type is the record's WorkerType, present on every entry whatever its source; this is an additive change.
4. list_workers. Comment the type field: one of the four values, as claim_next_task/join_team recorded it.

Leave MOE_WORKER_TYPE, proxy injection and launcher wording out. They belong to task-eb85340b and task-bdf6db6f, which document them once the code exists. No new error codes, so the Errors lists stay as they are.

Verification gate, run once. From the repository root, run exactly: (cd packages/moe-daemon && npx tsc --noEmit && npm test). It must exit 0. Submit the command, exitCode and outputTail (the vitest test-file and test totals) to moe.complete_task.

Environment:
- The shared checkout's node_modules are Windows-native, so under WSL vitest dies at startup with 'Cannot find native binding'. Never npm install in the shared checkout.
- Run the gate either from Windows Git Bash at the repo root, or in a Linux snapshot as described in Serena gotcha-daemon-linux-snapshot-gate:
  1. git archive HEAD of the WHOLE repo (initFiles.test.ts and the benchmarks read root files);
  2. overlay ONLY this task's five paths;
  3. symlink a native node_modules;
  4. run with MOE_* unset;
  5. cmp the five files against the worktree before citing the run.
- tsconfig excludes *.test.ts, so tsc does not type-check the new test file; vitest runs it.

This task changes no shared types, schema, migration or wire framing (one optional input field plus one additive output field), so this daemon suite is its full regression. The parent task-bdf6db6f re-runs the daemon and proxy suites together at the end.
